    # Dialogue 52: Code Refactoring & Testability
    [
        ("Can you help me refactor this messy transaction processing script into clean, maintainable Python code?\n\n```python\ndef process(data):\n    res = []\n    for d in data:\n        if d['status'] == 'completed':\n            if d['amount'] > 100:\n                fee = d['amount'] * 0.03\n            else:\n                fee = d['amount'] * 0.05\n            net = d['amount'] - fee\n            res.append({'id': d['id'], 'net': net, 'fee': fee})\n    return res\n```",
         "Here is a clean, idiomatic refactoring utilizing dataclasses, type hints, pure functions, and early filtering:\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import List, Dict, Any\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass ProcessedTransaction:\n    id: str\n    net: float\n    fee: float\n\ndef calculate_fee(amount: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Calculate transaction fee based on threshold tier.\"\"\"\n    fee_rate = 0.03 if amount > 100.0 else 0.05\n    return round(amount * fee_rate, 2)\n\ndef process_transaction(record: Dict[str, Any]) -> ProcessedTransaction:\n    \"\"\"Process a single completed transaction record into net and fee.\"\"\"\n    amount = float(record[\"amount\"])\n    fee = calculate_fee(amount)\n    net = round(amount - fee, 2)\n    return ProcessedTransaction(id=str(record[\"id\"]), net=net, fee=fee)\n\ndef process_completed_transactions(data: List[Dict[str, Any]]) -> List[ProcessedTransaction]:\n    \"\"\"Filter and process all completed transactions.\"\"\"\n    return [\n        process_transaction(record)\n        for record in data\n        if record.get(\"status\") == \"completed\"\n    ]\n```"),
        ("How does this refactoring make unit testing easier with `pytest`?",
         "By decoupling the fee logic into pure functions without side effects, you can test every edge case in complete isolation without constructing complex mock data structures:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\n\ndef test_calculate_fee_high_tier():\n    assert calculate_fee(200.0) == 6.0\n\ndef test_calculate_fee_low_tier():\n    assert calculate_fee(50.0) == 2.5\n\ndef test_calculate_fee_boundary():\n    assert calculate_fee(100.0) == 5.0  # verifies threshold <= 100 condition\n\ndef test_process_ignores_pending():\n    records = [\n        {\"id\": \"1\", \"status\": \"pending\", \"amount\": 200},\n        {\"id\": \"2\", \"status\": \"completed\", \"amount\": 200},\n    ]\n    results = process_completed_transactions(records)\n    assert len(results) == 1\n    assert results[0].id == \"2\"\n    assert results[0].net == 194.0\n```\nTests execute in microseconds and provide 100% boundary coverage.")
    ],

    # Dialogue 53: Production Incident Root Cause Analysis
    [
        ("Our production FastAPI service experienced a severe latency spike: p99 jumped from 45ms to 6,000ms after our latest deployment, and CPU spiked to 100%. What is the best root-cause analysis procedure?",
         "When an async Python service (FastAPI / Starlette / Uvicorn) experiences a sudden 100x latency spike accompanied by maxed CPU, the primary suspect is Event Loop Starvation caused by blocking synchronous calls.\n\nHere is the systematic diagnostic procedure:\n1. Inspect Git Diff: Review the latest deployed pull requests specifically for synchronous I/O libraries (`requests`, `urllib`, standard file I/O `open()`, synchronous DB drivers `psycopg2`, or CPU-bound JSON parsing `json.loads` on huge payloads).\n2. Generate Profiler Flame Graphs: Use `py-spy` or `austin` on a live staging worker under synthetic load:\n   `py-spy record -o profile.svg --pid <worker_pid>`\n3. Check Event Loop Lag: If an endpoint does blocking synchronous work inside an `async def` function, it freezes the single event loop thread, blocking ALL concurrent requests queued on that worker process."),
        ("We checked the git diff, and someone called `requests.get('https://external-auth-provider.com/verify')` inside an `async def verify_token()` dependency!",
         "That is the exact root cause! In FastAPI:\n- If you define a handler with `async def`, FastAPI runs it directly on the main event loop thread. Calling synchronous `requests.get()` blocks the entire thread for the duration of the network handshake and response (often 200-500ms). While that request waits, zero other async requests can make progress, creating an exponential queue backlog.\n\nTo fix it, switch to an asynchronous HTTP client like `httpx`:\n\n```python\nimport httpx\n\n# Reuse a persistent client across requests to keep connection pools warm\nclient = httpx.AsyncClient(timeout=5.0)\n\nasync def verify_token(token: str):\n    response = await client.get(\n        \"https://external-auth-provider.com/verify\",\n        headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {token}\"}\n    )\n    return response.json()\n```\nWith `await`, execution yields control back to the event loop while waiting for the network, restoring normal 45ms latency.")
    ],

